# Entity Framework Core Rules

## DbContext
- Use primary constructor: `public class AppDbContext(DbContextOptions<AppDbContext> options) : DbContext(options)`
- Apply all entity configurations via `modelBuilder.ApplyConfigurationsFromAssembly(...)`
- Use `DbSet<T>` properties with expression body: `public DbSet<Order> Orders => Set<Order>();`

## Entity Configuration
- Create a separate `IEntityTypeConfiguration<T>` class per entity
- Use Fluent API (avoid data annotations on domain entities)
- Map columns explicitly: `.HasColumnName()`, `.IsRequired()`, `.HasMaxLength()`
- Use `.HasConversion<string>()` for enum columns stored as text

## Querying
- Use `AsNoTracking()` for all read-only queries
- Use `AsSplitQuery()` when including multiple collection navigations
- Project to DTOs with `.Select()` to avoid over-fetching
- Use `FindAsync` for primary key lookups; use `FirstOrDefaultAsync` for others

## Migrations
```bash
# Add migration
dotnet ef migrations add <Name> --project src/MyApp.Infrastructure --startup-project src/MyApp.Api

# Apply migration
dotnet ef database update --project src/MyApp.Infrastructure --startup-project src/MyApp.Api

# Generate SQL script
dotnet ef migrations script --idempotent -o migrations.sql
```

## Repository Pattern
- Define repository interfaces in the Domain/Application layer
- Implement them in the Infrastructure layer using EF Core
- Never call `SaveChangesAsync` from a controller; call it from a repository or Unit of Work

## Testing
- Use Testcontainers with a real database provider for integration tests
- Apply migrations in test setup with `await context.Database.MigrateAsync()`
